  "id": "cretan_lyra",
  "name": "Cretan lyra (Cretan pear-shaped fiddle)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Cretan lyra solo-and-ensemble music tradition of Crete (Greece), centered on the pear-shaped three-string upright-played fiddle of unique Cretan construction (with the fingers stopping the strings from the side rather than pressing down, allowing characteristic Cretan-style pitch-glide ornament). Performance configuration: lyra leading with laouto long-neck-lute rhythmic-and-harmonic accompaniment, plus voice for sung repertoire. Repertoire spans the canonical Cretan dance-forms (pentozali fast-warrior-dance, syrtos slow-circle-dance, sousta), mantinada improvised-couplet form, and the rizitika hilltop-mountain-village ritual-song tradition. The post-twentieth-century Anogeia village and the Psarantonis-Xylouris family lineage anchor the canonical contemporary tradition. Distinguished from broader Greek nisiotika by Cretan-specific lyra-instrument signature.",
